//! This fixture runs a migration model on two processes. Each process binds
//! one declared world.
//!
//! A world names a process only through the binding of its host, never in the
//! source. Each migration to `far` runs in the process that binds `far`. The
//! two migrations hold and read one value at `far`.
//!
//! One process hosts `far` with `gandr host`, and another runs `main` with
//! `gandr run`. Each process takes the binding `--world far=<key>@<ip:port>`,
//! or the site and the manifold that the binding desugars from. The second
//! migration reads a capture that the hosting process holds already. So no
//! process fetches it. With no `--world` and no manifold, both migrations run
//! in this process and answer the same.
//!
//! - see: `crates/surface-driver/tests/net.rs`

/// The world the entry runs at.
pub world near;

/// The world the work runs at, in the process that its binding names.
pub world far;

/// `far` computes forty-two and holds it. A second migration reads it there
/// again.
@[entry(*{near, far, far}), runs("42")]
def main() -> Integer =
  let held : Integer at far = migrate [far] { (hold (6 * 7)) };
  let answer at far = held
  migrate [far] { pure answer }
